# Windmill stack secrets (services VM)
# Copy to .sops.env and encrypt with:
#   SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE=../../sops_age_key.txt sops -e -i .sops.env
#
# Key-set contract: this file, the real .sops.env and the test fixture
# tests/fixtures/windmill.sops.env must all carry EXACTLY the same keys — the
# stack-env-drift lint fails on any difference.
#
# Reminder from CLAUDE.md, verified empirically (finding #30): Compose
# INTERPOLATES env_file values, so any value containing '$' must be
# single-quoted in the plaintext before encrypting. Postgres passwords are a
# realistic source of '$' — generate with `openssl rand -hex`, which cannot
# emit one, or single-quote.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database. TWO keys holding the same password, because Windmill takes a full
# connection URL and the postgres container takes the password alone.
#
# DATABASE_URL is spelled out here rather than assembled with ${...} in
# compose.yaml on purpose: Compose would interpolate it wherever the
# decrypted .env does not exist yet (a deploy racing the decrypt, finding
# #11) and quietly render an empty password.
#
# ⚠ The user is `windmill`, not upstream's `postgres`. That is a deliberate
# deviation so nixos/backup-prepare.sh's loop (`docker exec windmill_db
# pg_dumpall -U windmill`) works; change one and you must change all three.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24
DATABASE_URL=postgres://windmill:changeme_openssl_rand_hex_24@windmill_db:5432/windmill?sslmode=disable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SUPERADMIN_SECRET — the virtual superadmin bearer token.
#
# 🚨 THIS IS WHAT KEEPS `admin@windmill.dev` / `changeme` FROM BEING LIVE.
# Windmill seeds that account and does NOT force a password change, so
# upstream's documented setup leaves a published default credential working
# until a human opens the UI. windmill_init presents this token instead,
# creates the real superadmin, and then DELETEs the default account.
#
# It rotates freely — it is a bearer token, not a KDF input — but treat it as a
# root password while it exists.
# Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SUPERADMIN_SECRET=changeme_openssl_rand_hex_32

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The real superadmin, created by windmill_init. The password must be at least
# 12 characters (the init script's own floor) and the email must not be
# admin@windmill.dev — the script refuses both rather than creating something
# that defeats the point.
# Generate the password: openssl rand -base64 24
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WINDMILL_ADMIN_EMAIL=idan@idanreed.com
WINDMILL_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme_strong_password
WINDMILL_WORKSPACE=main

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NO OIDC KEY HERE, and that is not an omission.
#
# Windmill's OSS build has no OIDC/OAuth login path — it is an Enterprise
# feature — so forward auth is the ONLY SSO available, and it is applied in
# stacks/caddy/Caddyfile rather than configured here. Its sharp edge is
# recorded there: Windmill's own session must still exist underneath, because
# forward auth cannot authenticate the worker's calls or any API client.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
